From 27df83cd67d9b29ce41e97f3beedd8de9b35ae31 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mjb-is <mjb-is@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 00:14:22 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 090/123] Back up and restore Windows creation times

pxar has no field for a file's creation time, so every restored file and
folder on Windows shows the time of the restore as its creation time
(TODO.md, "NTFS Metadata Fidelity").

Backup: the NTFS metadata collector records each file's and folder's
creation time in the side file next to the SDDL and attributes
(FileMetaEntry.Created, a FILETIME, json "c", omitted when 0). It comes
from the FileInfo the pxar walk already has, so there is no extra disk
access. The field is optional: older clients ignore it, and a snapshot
without it decodes with 0, which leaves the file alone.

Restore: on Windows the side file is fetched whether or not ACLs were
asked for, and after extraction each file and folder gets its creation
time back, before its attributes and ACL are applied (an ACL may take
away the right to change it). Only the creation time is written, so the
modification times the extraction set, folders' after their contents,
stay as restored. Linux and other systems have no settable creation time;
applyCreationTime is a no-op there.

Tests: TestCreationTimeRoundTrip (collector captures file and folder
creation times through the gzipped side file, applyCreationTime puts them
back to the 100 ns and leaves the modification time alone),
TestCreationTimeAbsentIsNoOp (a side file without "c"), and an opt-in live
test (PBS_E2E=1) that backs up a folder with old creation times and
restores it with and without ACLs.

